{
    id: "we-you",
    title: "We, you all, they: kami, kita, kamo, sila",
    blurb: "Choose the right we (with or without the listener), talk to a group, talk about others, and make nouns plural with mga.",
    deck: "People and family",
    body: `
<p>English "we" hides a question: does it include the person you are talking to? Cebuano always answers it, with two different words.</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>English</th><th>Full form</th><th>Short form</th></tr><tr><td>we (not you)</td><td class="k">kami</td><td class="k">mi</td></tr><tr><td>we (you and me)</td><td class="k">kita</td><td class="k">ta</td></tr><tr><td>you all</td><td class="k">kamo</td><td class="k">mo</td></tr><tr><td>they</td><td class="k">sila</td><td>none</td></tr></table></div>
<h3>kami or kita</h3>
<p>Telling a Cebuano "We're from the US" about you and your family? That is <b>kami</b>: the listener is left out. <b>kita</b> includes the listener: you and me, or all of us here.</p>
{{ex:Taga-Amerika mi.|tah-gah-ah-meh-ree-kah mee|We're from the US.}}
{{ex:Estudyante kita.|ehs-too-jahn-teh kee-TAH|We're students, you and I.}}
<h3>Short forms in second place</h3>
<p><b>mi</b>, <b>ta</b> and <b>mo</b> are the short forms of <b>kami</b>, <b>kita</b> and <b>kamo</b>. Like <b>ko</b> and <b>ka</b>, they never start a sentence; they go right after the main word or question word.</p>
{{ex:Taga-asa mo?|tah-gah-ah-sah moh|Where are you all from?}}
<h3>You all, and they</h3>
<p>English uses "you" for one person or for many. Cebuano uses <b>ikaw / ka</b> for one person and <b>kamo / mo</b> for two or more. <b>sila</b> is "they", for people.</p>
{{ex:Amerikano sila.|ah-meh-ree-kah-noh see-LAH|They're American.}}
<h3>More than one: mga</h3>
<p>Cebuano nouns never add -s. To show more than one, put <b>mga</b> (said "manga") in front: <b>ang mga bata</b>, "the children".</p>
{{ex:Mga maestro sila.|mah-ngah mah-ehs-troh see-LAH|They're teachers.}}
{{ex:Asa ang mga bata?|ah-sah ahng mah-ngah BAH-tahq|Where are the children?}}
<p>When a number or the context already shows more than one, <b>mga</b> is often not needed, so do not be surprised to hear <b>Amerikano sila</b> with no <b>mga</b>.</p>
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> <b>kita</b> for "my family and I" when you are talking to someone outside the family. <b>Taga-Amerika kita.</b> → <b>Taga-Amerika mi.</b></div>
`,
    check: [
      ["You tell a vendor We're from the US (you and your wife). Which do you say?", ["Taga-Amerika kita.", "Taga-Amerika mi.", "Taga-Amerika sila.", "Taga-Amerika ta."], 1, "The vendor is not included, so the we is kami, short form mi."],
      ["kamo means:", ["they", "we", "you all", "you (one person)"], 2, "kamo is you for two or more people."],
      ["How do you make bata (child) plural?", ["batas", "mga bata", "mga batas", "ang bata"], 1, "Put mga before the noun; Cebuano never adds -s."]
    ],
    practice: [
      ['mc', "we (you and I) =", "kita", "kami", "kamo", "sila", "kita includes the listener; kami leaves them out."],
      ['mc', "they =", "sila", "kamo", "kami", "siya", "sila is they; siya is he or she."],
      ['mc', "Which is the short form of kami?", "mi", "ta", "mo", "ka", "kami shortens to mi, kita to ta, kamo to mo."],
      ['mc', "the children =", "ang mga bata", "ang batas", "mga ang bata", "ang bata mga", "mga goes right before the noun: ang mga bata."],
      ['mc', "You ask two tourists where they are from:", "Taga-asa mo?", "Taga-asa ka?", "Taga-asa sila?", "Taga-asa mi?", "Two or more listeners are kamo, short form mo."],
      ['or', "We're from the US (not including you)", 'Taga-Amerika mi', 'ta', "The short pronoun mi (we, not you) goes in second place, after the main word."],
      ['or', "They're teachers", 'Mga maestro sila', 'nila', "The main word comes first, then sila: Mga maestro sila."],
      ['or', "We're students (you and I)", 'Estudyante kita', 'kami', "The main word comes first; kita includes the listener."],
      ['fi', 'Taga-Amerika __.', "We're from the US. (not including you)", 'mi|kami', 'ta|kita|sila', "kami or mi leaves the listener out."],
      ['fi', 'Estudyante __.', "You're students. (you all)", 'kamo|mo', 'ka|kami|sila', "kamo is you for two or more people."],
      ['fi', 'ang __ bata', "the children", 'mga', 'ka|si|sa', "mga makes the noun plural."],
      ['ty', "They're American.", 'Amerikano sila|Mga Amerikano sila', 'Amerikano · sila', 'nila', "The main word comes first, then sila: Amerikano sila."],
      ['ty', "We're from the US. (not including the listener)", 'Taga-Amerika mi|Taga-Amerika kami', 'taga- · Amerika · mi', 'kita', "The short pronoun mi goes in second place: Taga-Amerika mi."],
      ['ty', "Where are you all from?", 'Taga-asa mo|Taga-asa kamo', 'taga- · asa · mo', 'ka', "The short pronoun mo (you all) goes in second place, after taga-asa."]
    ]
  }
